window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2025-06-3", "source": "2025年06月真题·第3套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence \"With the increasing application of AI technology, there is a growing concern that it may negatively impact human creativity.\" You can make comments, cite examples or use your personal experiences to develop your essay. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words. You should copy the sentence given in quotes at the beginning of your essay.", "sample": "With the increasing application of AI technology, there is a growing concern that it may negatively impact\nhuman creativity. In my view, this worry is well-grounded, for excessive reliance on AI can gradually dull our\ncapacity to think and create independently.\nThe reason is straightforward. Creativity is a muscle that strengthens only through active practice. When we\nask AI to generate essays, ideas, or solutions at the click of a button, we skip the very struggle that sparks\noriginal thinking. Take my own experience as an example. Last semester, I turned to an AI tool whenever an\nassignment felt difficult. Although the drafts came quickly, I soon realized that my mind had grown lazy: I\ncould no longer brainstorm freely, and my writing lost its personal voice.\nTherefore, the concern deserves our serious attention. AI should serve as a helpful assistant rather than a\nreplacement for our own minds. Only by keeping our thinking active can we ensure that technology enriches,\nrather than erodes, human creativity."}, "translation": {"prompt": "自古以来,中国的水资源北缺南丰,分布极不均衡。为了有效解决北方严重缺水问题,中国政府实施了南水北调工程(the South-to-North Water Diversion Project)。这是一项跨区域配置水资源的宏大水利工程。历经数十年的规划与筹备,工程于2002年开工建设,分为东、中、西三条线路,总长4350公里,惠及人口将超过4亿。自2014年通水以来,工程向北方调水累计已超500亿立方米,为北方地区的人民提供了可靠的水资源,同时也极大地改善了这一地区的生态环境,促进了经济的可持续健康发展。", "sample": "Since ancient times, China's water resources have been unevenly distributed, with water scarce in the north but abundant in the south. To effectively address the severe water shortage in the north, the Chinese government launched the South-to-North Water Diversion Project. It is a massive cross-regional water conservancy project for allocating water resources. After decades of planning and preparation, the project began construction in 2002. Divided into eastern, central and western routes with a total length of 4,350 kilometers, it is expected to benefit more than 400 million people. Since the water supply began in 2014, the project has diverted over 50 billion cubic meters of water to the north, providing reliable water resources for people in the region, while greatly improving the local ecological environment and promoting sustainable and healthy economic development."}, "listeningUrl": null, "clozePassage": "The slow progress of women in elective office is frustrating for some political observers and experts—including myself. Achieving gender equality in Congress is an important goal. This is because the number of women involved in legislative decisions has significant  __26__  for all the policies that governments  __27__  . Female legislators are more likely than men to introduce, speak about and work to pass policies that disproportionately affect women and girls, such as paid family leave, pay  __28__  and gender-based violence.\nHaving more women in Congress also fosters a greater sense of connection between female voters and government. In addition, it  __29__  women's sense that government cares about their concerns and inspires young women to become more politically engaged.\nWhile women are underrepresented in governments around the globe, it is a particularly  __30__  problem in the United States. Currently, the U.S. ranks 73rd in the world when it comes to female representation in government.\nBut the reason women are so  __31__  underrepresented in U.S. government is not because they face resistance from voters or struggle to raise money. On the contrary, decades of research shows that female candidates raise as much money and win as often as male politicians with similar  __32__  .\nRather, the slow progress of women in politics is a tale of two political parties.\nIn the next Congress, there will be 107 female Democratic lawmakers and 42 female Republican lawmakers in the Senate and House combined.\nIn order for women to gain half of the seats in Congress, more women need to run,  __33__  on Republican tickets. This will require the Republican Party as a whole to  __34__  recruiting women—and not just for one election cycle, but in a  __35__  way.", "matchIntro": "The Benefits of Solitude", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "I can be a reluctant socialiser. I'm sometimes secretly pleased when social plans are called off. I get restless a few hours into a hangout. I even once went on a free 10-day silent meditation(冥想) retreat—not for the meditation, but for the silence."}, {"label": "B", "text": "So I can relate to author Anneli Rufus, who wrote in *Party of One: The Loners' Manifesto*: \"When parents on TV shows punished their kids by ordering them to go to their rooms, I was confused. I loved my room. Being there behind a locked door was a treat. To me a punishment was being ordered to play games with my cousin Louis.\""}, {"label": "C", "text": "Unsocial tendencies like these are often far from ideal. Abundant research shows the harms of social isolation, considered a serious public health problem in countries that have rapidly ageing populations (though talk of a 'loneliness epidemic' may be exaggerated). In the UK, the Royal College of General Practitioners says that loneliness has the same risk level for premature death as diabetes. Strong social connections are important for cognitive functioning, motor function and a smoothly running immune system."}, {"label": "D", "text": "This is especially clear from cases of extreme social isolation. Examples of people kept in captivity, children kept isolated in abusive orphanages, and prisoners kept in solitary confinement all show how prolonged solitude can lead to hallucinations(幻觉) and other forms of mental instability."}, {"label": "E", "text": "But these are severe and involuntary cases of aloneness. For those of us who just prefer plenty of alone time, emerging research suggests some good news: there are upsides to being alone—for both our work lives and our emotional well-being."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Solitude is a time for reflection, for thinking about things more deeply. In a world increasingly focused on speed and competitiveness, solitude is precious and something to be treasured. And there are benefits in learning to love your solitude."}, {"label": "G", "text": "One key benefit of solitude is improved creativity. Gregory Feist, who focuses on the psychology of creativity at California's San Jose State University, has defined creativity as thinking or activity with two key elements: originality and usefulness. He has found that personality traits commonly associated with creativity are openness (receptiveness to new thoughts and experiences), self-confidence, and autonomy—which may include \"a lack of concern for social norms\" and \"a preference for being alone\". In fact, Feist's research on both artists and scientists shows that one of the most prominent features of creative folks is their lesser interest in socialising."}, {"label": "H", "text": "One reason for this is that such people are likely to spend sustained time alone working on their craft. Plus, Feist says, many artists \"are trying to make sense of their internal world and a lot of internal personal experiences that they're trying to give expression and meaning to through their art.\" Solitude allows for the reflection and observation necessary for that creative process."}, {"label": "I", "text": "A recent justification of these ideas came from University at Buffalo psychologist Julie Bowker, who researches social withdrawal. Social withdrawal is usually categorised into three types: shyness caused by fear or anxiety; avoidance, from a dislike of socialising; and unsociability, from a preference for solitude. A paper by Bowker and her colleagues was the first to show that a type of social withdrawal could have a positive effect—they found that creativity was linked specifically to unsociability. They also found that unsociability had no correlation with aggression (shyness and avoidance did). This was significant because while previous research had suggested that unsociability might be harmless, Bowker and colleagues' paper showed that it could actually be beneficial. Unsociable people are likely to be \"having just enough interaction,\" Bowker says. \"They have a preference for being alone, but they also don't mind being with others.\""}, {"label": "J", "text": "Still, it turns out that solitude is important for more than creativity. It's commonly believed that leaders need to be sociable. But this depends, among other things, on the personalities of their employees. One 2011 study showed that in branches of a pizza chain where employees were more passive, outgoing bosses were associated with higher profits. But in branches where employees were more active, introverted(内向的) leaders were more effective. One reason for this is that introverted people are less likely to feel threatened by strong personalities and suggestions. They're also more likely to listen."}, {"label": "K", "text": "Since ancient times, meanwhile, people have been aware of a link between isolation and mental focus. After all, cultures with traditions of religious hermits(隐士) believe that solitude is important for enlightenment. Recent research has given us a better understanding of why. One benefit of unsociability is the brain's state of active mental rest, which goes hand-in-hand with the stillness of being alone. When another person is present, your brain can't help but pay some attention. This can be a positive distraction. But it's still a distraction."}, {"label": "L", "text": "Daydreaming in the absence of such distractions activates the brain's default-mode network. Among other functions, this network helps to consolidate memory and understand others' emotions. Giving free rein to a wandering mind not only helps with focus in the long term but strengthens your sense of both yourself and others. Paradoxically, therefore, periods of solitude actually help when it comes time to socialise once more. And the occasional absence of focus ultimately helps concentration in the long run."}, {"label": "M", "text": "A more recent advocate of thoughtful and productive solitude is Susan Cain, author of *Quiet: The Power of Introverts in a World That Can't Stop Talking* and founder of Quiet Revolution, a company that promotes quiet and introvert-friendly workplaces. \"These days, we tend to believe that creativity emerges from a decidedly unreserved socialising process, but in fact it requires sustained attention and deep focus,\" she says. \"Also, humans are such absorbent social beings that when we surround ourselves with others, we automatically take in their opinions and aesthetics. To truly chart our own path or vision, we have to be willing to isolate ourselves, at least for some period of time.\""}, {"label": "N", "text": "Still, the line between useful solitude and dangerous isolation can be blurry. \"Almost anything can be adaptive and maladaptive, depending on how extreme they get,\" Feist says. A disorder has to do with dysfunction. If someone stops caring about people and cuts off all contact, this could point to an irrational neglect of social relations. But creative unsociability is a far cry from this. In fact, Feist says, \"there's a real danger with people who are never alone.\" It's hard to examine one's own thoughts, feelings and motives, to be self-aware, and fully relaxed unless you have occasional solitude. In addition, introverts tend to have fewer but stronger friendships—which has been linked to greater happiness."}, {"label": "O", "text": "As with many things, quality reigns over quantity. Nurturing a few solid relationships without feeling the need to constantly populate your life with endless chatting ultimately may be better for you."}, {"label": "P", "text": "Thus, if your personality tends toward unsociability, you shouldn't feel the need to change. Of course, that comes with qualifications. But as long as you have regular social contact, you are choosing solitude rather than being forced into it, you have at least a few good friends and your solitude is good for your well-being or productivity, there's no point agonising over how to fit a square personality into a round hole."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Simulators are most often utilized within industries such as nuclear power, aviation and surgery where failure results in disastrous consequences. To maximize the value from a simulation learning experience, participants should immediately and directly apply their learning to a specific intervention within their organization.\nMost organizations aspire to deploy significant change programs, only to find them nearly impossible to implement. That is largely because successful change requires more than a vision, it requires a workforce that not only doesn't resist change, but embraces it.\nTo achieve success, an organization must build a transformation program that will allow change to be rapidly pulled across its departments and throughout its layers. Regardless of the level of senior management commitment, unless key thought leaders at all levels embrace the change, the initiative will wither and die. To create this kind of widespread passion, learning leaders must expose the workforce to what could be, which will enable them to rethink their mental models, enable them to break free from their deep-rooted paradigms and embrace the opportunity to learn.\nAllowing participants to enter a simulated environment provides them with the opportunity to experience alternative realities which can prompt them to rethink their current beliefs.\nBehavioral change is not easy for most adults. Lectures, training programs and workshops can explain the intellectual elements of transformation, but they are seldom effective at getting to the behavioral aspects that lie at the heart of a significant change initiative. Further, under normal working conditions, managers rarely see the full effect of their employee development efforts. As such, an intervention like a simulation can provide the stimulus for change.\nAn effective simulation can be better than experience as a learning tool because it accelerates time, compresses space, and unlike reality, is specifically designed to maximize participant learning. Simulations provide an immersive learning experience where skills, processes and knowledge all can be highlighted in a way reality cannot. The ability to explore, experiment and repeatedly apply new knowledge in unlimited, risk-free models is what makes simulation one of the most productive forms of learning.\nWell-designed simulations can enable individuals and groups to develop a deep level of understanding about how their decisions and intuitive responses to business stimuli affect their fellow participants and the organization as a whole. To reap the benefits, however, simulations must feel like reality. At the end of the successful simulation, participants must declare \"this is us.\" If they don't, they will view the experience as a game, which can be difficult to apply on the job, or worse, irrelevant to everyday work tasks. To maximize benefits from simulation, participants should immediately apply the learning from the experience to forge a smooth link between learning and doing.", "47": "Simulators are most often utilized within industries such as nuclear power, aviation and surgery where failure results in disastrous consequences. To maximize the value from a simulation learning experience, participants should immediately and directly apply their learning to a specific intervention within their organization.\nMost organizations aspire to deploy significant change programs, only to find them nearly impossible to implement. That is largely because successful change requires more than a vision, it requires a workforce that not only doesn't resist change, but embraces it.\nTo achieve success, an organization must build a transformation program that will allow change to be rapidly pulled across its departments and throughout its layers. Regardless of the level of senior management commitment, unless key thought leaders at all levels embrace the change, the initiative will wither and die. To create this kind of widespread passion, learning leaders must expose the workforce to what could be, which will enable them to rethink their mental models, enable them to break free from their deep-rooted paradigms and embrace the opportunity to learn.\nAllowing participants to enter a simulated environment provides them with the opportunity to experience alternative realities which can prompt them to rethink their current beliefs.\nBehavioral change is not easy for most adults. Lectures, training programs and workshops can explain the intellectual elements of transformation, but they are seldom effective at getting to the behavioral aspects that lie at the heart of a significant change initiative. Further, under normal working conditions, managers rarely see the full effect of their employee development efforts. As such, an intervention like a simulation can provide the stimulus for change.\nAn effective simulation can be better than experience as a learning tool because it accelerates time, compresses space, and unlike reality, is specifically designed to maximize participant learning. Simulations provide an immersive learning experience where skills, processes and knowledge all can be highlighted in a way reality cannot. The ability to explore, experiment and repeatedly apply new knowledge in unlimited, risk-free models is what makes simulation one of the most productive forms of learning.\nWell-designed simulations can enable individuals and groups to develop a deep level of understanding about how their decisions and intuitive responses to business stimuli affect their fellow participants and the organization as a whole. To reap the benefits, however, simulations must feel like reality. At the end of the successful simulation, participants must declare \"this is us.\" If they don't, they will view the experience as a game, which can be difficult to apply on the job, or worse, irrelevant to everyday work tasks. To maximize benefits from simulation, participants should immediately apply the learning from the experience to forge a smooth link between learning and doing.", "48": "Simulators are most often utilized within industries such as nuclear power, aviation and surgery where failure results in disastrous consequences. To maximize the value from a simulation learning experience, participants should immediately and directly apply their learning to a specific intervention within their organization.\nMost organizations aspire to deploy significant change programs, only to find them nearly impossible to implement. That is largely because successful change requires more than a vision, it requires a workforce that not only doesn't resist change, but embraces it.\nTo achieve success, an organization must build a transformation program that will allow change to be rapidly pulled across its departments and throughout its layers. Regardless of the level of senior management commitment, unless key thought leaders at all levels embrace the change, the initiative will wither and die. To create this kind of widespread passion, learning leaders must expose the workforce to what could be, which will enable them to rethink their mental models, enable them to break free from their deep-rooted paradigms and embrace the opportunity to learn.\nAllowing participants to enter a simulated environment provides them with the opportunity to experience alternative realities which can prompt them to rethink their current beliefs.\nBehavioral change is not easy for most adults. Lectures, training programs and workshops can explain the intellectual elements of transformation, but they are seldom effective at getting to the behavioral aspects that lie at the heart of a significant change initiative. Further, under normal working conditions, managers rarely see the full effect of their employee development efforts. As such, an intervention like a simulation can provide the stimulus for change.\nAn effective simulation can be better than experience as a learning tool because it accelerates time, compresses space, and unlike reality, is specifically designed to maximize participant learning. Simulations provide an immersive learning experience where skills, processes and knowledge all can be highlighted in a way reality cannot. The ability to explore, experiment and repeatedly apply new knowledge in unlimited, risk-free models is what makes simulation one of the most productive forms of learning.\nWell-designed simulations can enable individuals and groups to develop a deep level of understanding about how their decisions and intuitive responses to business stimuli affect their fellow participants and the organization as a whole. To reap the benefits, however, simulations must feel like reality. At the end of the successful simulation, participants must declare \"this is us.\" If they don't, they will view the experience as a game, which can be difficult to apply on the job, or worse, irrelevant to everyday work tasks. To maximize benefits from simulation, participants should immediately apply the learning from the experience to forge a smooth link between learning and doing.", "49": "Simulators are most often utilized within industries such as nuclear power, aviation and surgery where failure results in disastrous consequences. To maximize the value from a simulation learning experience, participants should immediately and directly apply their learning to a specific intervention within their organization.\nMost organizations aspire to deploy significant change programs, only to find them nearly impossible to implement. That is largely because successful change requires more than a vision, it requires a workforce that not only doesn't resist change, but embraces it.\nTo achieve success, an organization must build a transformation program that will allow change to be rapidly pulled across its departments and throughout its layers. Regardless of the level of senior management commitment, unless key thought leaders at all levels embrace the change, the initiative will wither and die. To create this kind of widespread passion, learning leaders must expose the workforce to what could be, which will enable them to rethink their mental models, enable them to break free from their deep-rooted paradigms and embrace the opportunity to learn.\nAllowing participants to enter a simulated environment provides them with the opportunity to experience alternative realities which can prompt them to rethink their current beliefs.\nBehavioral change is not easy for most adults. Lectures, training programs and workshops can explain the intellectual elements of transformation, but they are seldom effective at getting to the behavioral aspects that lie at the heart of a significant change initiative. Further, under normal working conditions, managers rarely see the full effect of their employee development efforts. As such, an intervention like a simulation can provide the stimulus for change.\nAn effective simulation can be better than experience as a learning tool because it accelerates time, compresses space, and unlike reality, is specifically designed to maximize participant learning. Simulations provide an immersive learning experience where skills, processes and knowledge all can be highlighted in a way reality cannot. The ability to explore, experiment and repeatedly apply new knowledge in unlimited, risk-free models is what makes simulation one of the most productive forms of learning.\nWell-designed simulations can enable individuals and groups to develop a deep level of understanding about how their decisions and intuitive responses to business stimuli affect their fellow participants and the organization as a whole. To reap the benefits, however, simulations must feel like reality. At the end of the successful simulation, participants must declare \"this is us.\" If they don't, they will view the experience as a game, which can be difficult to apply on the job, or worse, irrelevant to everyday work tasks. To maximize benefits from simulation, participants should immediately apply the learning from the experience to forge a smooth link between learning and doing.", "50": "Simulators are most often utilized within industries such as nuclear power, aviation and surgery where failure results in disastrous consequences. To maximize the value from a simulation learning experience, participants should immediately and directly apply their learning to a specific intervention within their organization.\nMost organizations aspire to deploy significant change programs, only to find them nearly impossible to implement. That is largely because successful change requires more than a vision, it requires a workforce that not only doesn't resist change, but embraces it.\nTo achieve success, an organization must build a transformation program that will allow change to be rapidly pulled across its departments and throughout its layers. Regardless of the level of senior management commitment, unless key thought leaders at all levels embrace the change, the initiative will wither and die. To create this kind of widespread passion, learning leaders must expose the workforce to what could be, which will enable them to rethink their mental models, enable them to break free from their deep-rooted paradigms and embrace the opportunity to learn.\nAllowing participants to enter a simulated environment provides them with the opportunity to experience alternative realities which can prompt them to rethink their current beliefs.\nBehavioral change is not easy for most adults. Lectures, training programs and workshops can explain the intellectual elements of transformation, but they are seldom effective at getting to the behavioral aspects that lie at the heart of a significant change initiative. Further, under normal working conditions, managers rarely see the full effect of their employee development efforts. As such, an intervention like a simulation can provide the stimulus for change.\nAn effective simulation can be better than experience as a learning tool because it accelerates time, compresses space, and unlike reality, is specifically designed to maximize participant learning. Simulations provide an immersive learning experience where skills, processes and knowledge all can be highlighted in a way reality cannot. The ability to explore, experiment and repeatedly apply new knowledge in unlimited, risk-free models is what makes simulation one of the most productive forms of learning.\nWell-designed simulations can enable individuals and groups to develop a deep level of understanding about how their decisions and intuitive responses to business stimuli affect their fellow participants and the organization as a whole. To reap the benefits, however, simulations must feel like reality. At the end of the successful simulation, participants must declare \"this is us.\" If they don't, they will view the experience as a game, which can be difficult to apply on the job, or worse, irrelevant to everyday work tasks. To maximize benefits from simulation, participants should immediately apply the learning from the experience to forge a smooth link between learning and doing.", "51": "GDP growth is not a good indicator of how well a country is performing, and should not be the primary goal of governments. Unlimited growth is not sustainable, and economic thinking is moving toward the idea that we should aim for sustainability in our economic models. But while a sustainable economy is vital to our future, it is a means to an end, not an end in itself.\nThe idea that governments should focus on happiness has its critics. There are concerns about how happiness can be measured. Is happiness not a fleeting and subjective psychological state? Don't different people experience different levels of happiness? Even on the broadest interpretation of 'happiness' as prosperity or 'life satisfaction', people want different things.\nOf course, governments cannot impose life satisfaction on citizens. But our happiness relies on collaborative efforts as a society. A government's obligation lies in creating conditions that promote prosperity. And there is good reason to suppose that such conditions exist, are globally applicable, and are discoverable through research.\nIn a recently published article, philosopher Julian Baggini suggests we should focus on 'real wealth' for citizens, which does not depend on GDP growth. Access is key: people do not need to own, but rather access things that enable them to live well. Technological advances and changes in social behavior enable us to make more efficient use of the assets that we already have. And focusing on access to the resources people need to live better lives could help reduce inequality.\nAs far as it goes, this has much in common with proposals tabled by 'happiness' advocates. But it sets the bar far too low for what governments can and should be doing for their citizens.\nFor example, it's not clear how a 'real wealth' economy would remedy the epidemic of mental ill-health that plagues our society. In Western countries, at least—poor mental health is more detrimental to wellbeing than poverty. Over and above a vastly improved provision of therapeutic mental healthcare, there are preventative measures for improving mental health that governments could and should adopt. The WHO recommends establishing institutions that facilitate community participation—educational programs and interventions that provide skills for promoting mental wellbeing. It says a lot, however, that the WHO feels the need to appeal to the economic benefits of improving mental health to persuade governments that the cost of taking proposed measures is justified. As long as the economy is their priority, governments need go no further than ensuring citizens' continued productivity.\nTo demand that governments set the 'happiness' of citizens as their highest priority is to demand that they view citizens as ends in themselves.", "52": "GDP growth is not a good indicator of how well a country is performing, and should not be the primary goal of governments. Unlimited growth is not sustainable, and economic thinking is moving toward the idea that we should aim for sustainability in our economic models. But while a sustainable economy is vital to our future, it is a means to an end, not an end in itself.\nThe idea that governments should focus on happiness has its critics. There are concerns about how happiness can be measured. Is happiness not a fleeting and subjective psychological state? Don't different people experience different levels of happiness? Even on the broadest interpretation of 'happiness' as prosperity or 'life satisfaction', people want different things.\nOf course, governments cannot impose life satisfaction on citizens. But our happiness relies on collaborative efforts as a society. A government's obligation lies in creating conditions that promote prosperity. And there is good reason to suppose that such conditions exist, are globally applicable, and are discoverable through research.\nIn a recently published article, philosopher Julian Baggini suggests we should focus on 'real wealth' for citizens, which does not depend on GDP growth. Access is key: people do not need to own, but rather access things that enable them to live well. Technological advances and changes in social behavior enable us to make more efficient use of the assets that we already have. And focusing on access to the resources people need to live better lives could help reduce inequality.\nAs far as it goes, this has much in common with proposals tabled by 'happiness' advocates. But it sets the bar far too low for what governments can and should be doing for their citizens.\nFor example, it's not clear how a 'real wealth' economy would remedy the epidemic of mental ill-health that plagues our society. In Western countries, at least—poor mental health is more detrimental to wellbeing than poverty. Over and above a vastly improved provision of therapeutic mental healthcare, there are preventative measures for improving mental health that governments could and should adopt. The WHO recommends establishing institutions that facilitate community participation—educational programs and interventions that provide skills for promoting mental wellbeing. It says a lot, however, that the WHO feels the need to appeal to the economic benefits of improving mental health to persuade governments that the cost of taking proposed measures is justified. As long as the economy is their priority, governments need go no further than ensuring citizens' continued productivity.\nTo demand that governments set the 'happiness' of citizens as their highest priority is to demand that they view citizens as ends in themselves.", "53": "GDP growth is not a good indicator of how well a country is performing, and should not be the primary goal of governments. Unlimited growth is not sustainable, and economic thinking is moving toward the idea that we should aim for sustainability in our economic models. But while a sustainable economy is vital to our future, it is a means to an end, not an end in itself.\nThe idea that governments should focus on happiness has its critics. There are concerns about how happiness can be measured. Is happiness not a fleeting and subjective psychological state? Don't different people experience different levels of happiness? Even on the broadest interpretation of 'happiness' as prosperity or 'life satisfaction', people want different things.\nOf course, governments cannot impose life satisfaction on citizens. But our happiness relies on collaborative efforts as a society. A government's obligation lies in creating conditions that promote prosperity. And there is good reason to suppose that such conditions exist, are globally applicable, and are discoverable through research.\nIn a recently published article, philosopher Julian Baggini suggests we should focus on 'real wealth' for citizens, which does not depend on GDP growth. Access is key: people do not need to own, but rather access things that enable them to live well. Technological advances and changes in social behavior enable us to make more efficient use of the assets that we already have. And focusing on access to the resources people need to live better lives could help reduce inequality.\nAs far as it goes, this has much in common with proposals tabled by 'happiness' advocates. But it sets the bar far too low for what governments can and should be doing for their citizens.\nFor example, it's not clear how a 'real wealth' economy would remedy the epidemic of mental ill-health that plagues our society. In Western countries, at least—poor mental health is more detrimental to wellbeing than poverty. Over and above a vastly improved provision of therapeutic mental healthcare, there are preventative measures for improving mental health that governments could and should adopt. The WHO recommends establishing institutions that facilitate community participation—educational programs and interventions that provide skills for promoting mental wellbeing. It says a lot, however, that the WHO feels the need to appeal to the economic benefits of improving mental health to persuade governments that the cost of taking proposed measures is justified. As long as the economy is their priority, governments need go no further than ensuring citizens' continued productivity.\nTo demand that governments set the 'happiness' of citizens as their highest priority is to demand that they view citizens as ends in themselves.", "54": "GDP growth is not a good indicator of how well a country is performing, and should not be the primary goal of governments. Unlimited growth is not sustainable, and economic thinking is moving toward the idea that we should aim for sustainability in our economic models. But while a sustainable economy is vital to our future, it is a means to an end, not an end in itself.\nThe idea that governments should focus on happiness has its critics. There are concerns about how happiness can be measured. Is happiness not a fleeting and subjective psychological state? Don't different people experience different levels of happiness? Even on the broadest interpretation of 'happiness' as prosperity or 'life satisfaction', people want different things.\nOf course, governments cannot impose life satisfaction on citizens. But our happiness relies on collaborative efforts as a society. A government's obligation lies in creating conditions that promote prosperity. And there is good reason to suppose that such conditions exist, are globally applicable, and are discoverable through research.\nIn a recently published article, philosopher Julian Baggini suggests we should focus on 'real wealth' for citizens, which does not depend on GDP growth. Access is key: people do not need to own, but rather access things that enable them to live well. Technological advances and changes in social behavior enable us to make more efficient use of the assets that we already have. And focusing on access to the resources people need to live better lives could help reduce inequality.\nAs far as it goes, this has much in common with proposals tabled by 'happiness' advocates. But it sets the bar far too low for what governments can and should be doing for their citizens.\nFor example, it's not clear how a 'real wealth' economy would remedy the epidemic of mental ill-health that plagues our society. In Western countries, at least—poor mental health is more detrimental to wellbeing than poverty. Over and above a vastly improved provision of therapeutic mental healthcare, there are preventative measures for improving mental health that governments could and should adopt. The WHO recommends establishing institutions that facilitate community participation—educational programs and interventions that provide skills for promoting mental wellbeing. It says a lot, however, that the WHO feels the need to appeal to the economic benefits of improving mental health to persuade governments that the cost of taking proposed measures is justified. As long as the economy is their priority, governments need go no further than ensuring citizens' continued productivity.\nTo demand that governments set the 'happiness' of citizens as their highest priority is to demand that they view citizens as ends in themselves.", "55": "GDP growth is not a good indicator of how well a country is performing, and should not be the primary goal of governments. Unlimited growth is not sustainable, and economic thinking is moving toward the idea that we should aim for sustainability in our economic models. But while a sustainable economy is vital to our future, it is a means to an end, not an end in itself.\nThe idea that governments should focus on happiness has its critics. There are concerns about how happiness can be measured. Is happiness not a fleeting and subjective psychological state? Don't different people experience different levels of happiness? Even on the broadest interpretation of 'happiness' as prosperity or 'life satisfaction', people want different things.\nOf course, governments cannot impose life satisfaction on citizens. But our happiness relies on collaborative efforts as a society. A government's obligation lies in creating conditions that promote prosperity. And there is good reason to suppose that such conditions exist, are globally applicable, and are discoverable through research.\nIn a recently published article, philosopher Julian Baggini suggests we should focus on 'real wealth' for citizens, which does not depend on GDP growth. Access is key: people do not need to own, but rather access things that enable them to live well. Technological advances and changes in social behavior enable us to make more efficient use of the assets that we already have. And focusing on access to the resources people need to live better lives could help reduce inequality.\nAs far as it goes, this has much in common with proposals tabled by 'happiness' advocates. But it sets the bar far too low for what governments can and should be doing for their citizens.\nFor example, it's not clear how a 'real wealth' economy would remedy the epidemic of mental ill-health that plagues our society. In Western countries, at least—poor mental health is more detrimental to wellbeing than poverty. Over and above a vastly improved provision of therapeutic mental healthcare, there are preventative measures for improving mental health that governments could and should adopt. The WHO recommends establishing institutions that facilitate community participation—educational programs and interventions that provide skills for promoting mental wellbeing. It says a lot, however, that the WHO feels the need to appeal to the economic benefits of improving mental health to persuade governments that the cost of taking proposed measures is justified. As long as the economy is their priority, governments need go no further than ensuring citizens' continued productivity.\nTo demand that governments set the 'happiness' of citizens as their highest priority is to demand that they view citizens as ends in themselves."}, "questions": [{"id": "2025-06-3-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) bolsters", "B) consequences", "C) credentials", "D) dramatically", "E) enact", "F) equity", "G) especially", "H) evasively", "I) formidable", "J) impetus", "K) lavish", "L) prioritize", "M) suffices", "N) sustained", "O) tenured"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) consequences\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 significant + ___ + for 介词短语\n【依据】have consequences for 是高频固定搭配，意为'对…产生影响'\n本句解释'女性议员数量对所有政策都有重要影响'，与 Female legislators are more likely\nthan men to introduce, speak about and work to pass policies 一脉相承，\nconsequences for policies 完全贴合。\n【竞争词】C) credentials '资历'与 for policies 搭配不成立\ncredentials 指个人证书、资格，对应的是 candidate 本身，不能说'议员数量对政策\n有 credentials'。\nF) equity 义为'公平'，与 have ... for 搭不成立\nhave significant equity for policies 既不成搭配，也不通逻辑。\nJ) impetus '推动力'常用 give/provide impetus to，不用 have impetus for\nimpetus 与 for 共现表'对…的推动'虽可，但句意偏'对政策有影响'而非'推动'，consequences\n更贴。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 J) —— impetus 看似也能与 for 搭，'对…的推动'',词性也对。考生\n易被 has significant impetus for policies 的字面看似通顺骗到；防坑点是 have\nconsequences for 才是英语里'对…有影响'的固定搭配，且本段紧接的例证 Female\nlegislators are more likely than men to introduce, speak about and work to pass\npolicies 说的是'影响政策内容'而非'推动政策出现'。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·形容词 significant + __"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) bolsters", "B) consequences", "C) credentials", "D) dramatically", "E) enact", "F) equity", "G) especially", "H) evasively", "I) formidable", "J) impetus", "K) lavish", "L) prioritize", "M) suffices", "N) sustained", "O) tenured"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) enact\n动词\n【词性槽】定语从句 that governments + ___，缺谓语动词\n【依据】enact policies 是固定搭配'颁布/制定政策'\n上文谈'女性议员推动政策通过' work to pass policies that disproportionately affect\nwomen and girls，governments enact policies 与之同义，且 enact 与 policies/laws 是\n公认搭配。\n【竞争词】L) prioritize '优先处理'语义偏'排序'，且要被 34 题占用\nprioritize policies 也能说，但语义在'优先排序'而非'制定'；而且 34 题需要 prioritize 与\nrecruiting 搭配，词只能用一次。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·定语从句 that government"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) bolsters", "B) consequences", "C) credentials", "D) dramatically", "E) enact", "F) equity", "G) especially", "H) evasively", "I) formidable", "J) impetus", "K) lavish", "L) prioritize", "M) suffices", "N) sustained", "O) tenured"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) equity\n名词\n【词性槽】并列名词项 paid family leave, pay ___ and gender-based violence\n【依据】pay equity = 同工同酬，影响女性的典型议题\n本段例证 policies that disproportionately affect women and girls 列举与女性相关的政\n策议题，pay equity（同工同酬）与 paid family leave、gender-based violence 同属'女\n性权益'范畴，是英语世界讨论性别平等的高频术语。\n【竞争词】J) impetus pay impetus 不成搭配，且与女性议题无关\nimpetus 是'推动力'，不与 pay 构成复合名词，也无法作为'议题'与 paid family leave 并列。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·并列名词项 paid family le"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) bolsters", "B) consequences", "C) credentials", "D) dramatically", "E) enact", "F) equity", "G) especially", "H) evasively", "I) formidable", "J) impetus", "K) lavish", "L) prioritize", "M) suffices", "N) sustained", "O) tenured"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) bolsters\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 it + ___ + 宾语 women's sense\n【依据】bolster one's sense 是'增强某人的…感'的常见搭配\n上文 Having more women in Congress also fosters a greater sense of connection 已\n用 foster；本句用并列动词继续推进同向语义，bolsters women's sense that\ngovernment cares 自然衔接。\n【竞争词】M) suffices suffices 是不及物动词，不能带宾语\nsuffice 表'足够'，常说 it suffices to do/that...，后面跟从句或不定式；不能直接接 women's\nsense 作宾语，语法不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·主语 it + ___ + 宾语 wom"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) bolsters", "B) consequences", "C) credentials", "D) dramatically", "E) enact", "F) equity", "G) especially", "H) evasively", "I) formidable", "J) impetus", "K) lavish", "L) prioritize", "M) suffices", "N) sustained", "O) tenured"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) formidable\n形容词\n【词性槽】不定冠词 a + 副词 particularly + ___ + 名词 problem\n【依据】与 problem 构成常见搭配'棘手的问题'\n上下文交代美国排名第 73 the U.S. ranks 73rd in the world，说明这一问题在美国'尤其棘\n手'；formidable problem 是英语高频搭配，与 particularly 程度副词自然衔接。\n【竞争词】K) lavish 词义指向'奢华、铺张'，与 problem 不搭\nlavish 多形容花费、款待，无法描述社会问题的严重程度。\nO) tenured 义为'终身聘任的'，专用于职位\ntenured 常见搭配是 tenured professor/position，修饰 problem 完全不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·不定冠词 a + 副词 particul"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) bolsters", "B) consequences", "C) credentials", "D) dramatically", "E) enact", "F) equity", "G) especially", "H) evasively", "I) formidable", "J) impetus", "K) lavish", "L) prioritize", "M) suffices", "N) sustained", "O) tenured"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) dramatically\n副词\n【词性槽】be 动词 are + ___ + 过去分词 underrepresented\n【依据】强调'代表性偏低'的程度，呼应美国排第 73\n上文已用 particularly formidable 渲染问题严重，本句继续追问'为何如此严重地代表不\n足'；dramatically underrepresented 是英文报道里固定的'程度极大'表达。\n【竞争词】G) especially 意为'尤其'，常引出比较或例子\nespecially 通常需要一个比较对象或后接具体范畴（especially on Republican tickets 那种），\n单独修饰 underrepresented 表程度则不自然，且 G 已被 33 题强需占用。\nH) evasively 义为'回避地、含糊地'，与 underrepresented 逻辑不通\nevasively 多修饰 answer/respond 等言语动词，描述'代表性不足'毫无语义关联。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·be 动词 are + ___ + 过去"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) bolsters", "B) consequences", "C) credentials", "D) dramatically", "E) enact", "F) equity", "G) especially", "H) evasively", "I) formidable", "J) impetus", "K) lavish", "L) prioritize", "M) suffices", "N) sustained", "O) tenured"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) credentials\n名词\n【词性槽】介词 with + 形容词 similar + ___\n【依据】candidates with similar credentials = '资历相当的候选人'\n本段反驳一种误解 not because they face resistance from voters or struggle to raise\nmoney，再以研究为据 female candidates raise as much money and win as often as\nmale politicians with similar __(32)__. 进行同等条件下的对比，credentials（资历）正是\n这种'公平比较'的基准。\n【竞争词】J) impetus '推动力'不可用 similar 衡量，且与候选人能力比较无关\nsimilar impetus 在政客比较语境下毫无意义，无法构成对照基准。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·介词 with + 形容词 simila"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) bolsters", "B) consequences", "C) credentials", "D) dramatically", "E) enact", "F) equity", "G) especially", "H) evasively", "I) formidable", "J) impetus", "K) lavish", "L) prioritize", "M) suffices", "N) sustained", "O) tenured"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) especially\n副词\n【词性槽】逗号后 + ___ + 介词短语 on Republican tickets\n【依据】聚焦到共和党票上，呼应'两党之别'主旨\n上文 there will be 107 female Democratic lawmakers and 42 female Republican\nlawmakers 表明共和党女议员人数远少；本句强调 more women need to run, especially\non Republican tickets，正是把'更多女性参选'的火力点向共和党聚焦。\n【竞争词】H) evasively 义不搭\nevasively 描述'回避地、含糊地'参选毫无意义，逻辑指向反。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·逗号后 + ___ + 介词短语 on"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) bolsters", "B) consequences", "C) credentials", "D) dramatically", "E) enact", "F) equity", "G) especially", "H) evasively", "I) formidable", "J) impetus", "K) lavish", "L) prioritize", "M) suffices", "N) sustained", "O) tenured"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) prioritize\n动词\n【词性槽】情态动词 to + ___ + 动名词 recruiting\n【依据】prioritize doing 是'优先去做某事'的固定搭配\n上文已点出共和党女议员稀少 42 female Republican lawmakers，本句号召共和党把'招募\n女性'放到优先级首位，prioritize recruiting women 既合搭配也合语义。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·情态动词 to + ___ + 动名词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) bolsters", "B) consequences", "C) credentials", "D) dramatically", "E) enact", "F) equity", "G) especially", "H) evasively", "I) formidable", "J) impetus", "K) lavish", "L) prioritize", "M) suffices", "N) sustained", "O) tenured"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) sustained\n形容词\n【词性槽】介词 in + 限定词 a + ___ + 名词 way\n【依据】与 not just for one election cycle 形成时间维度的对照——'持续的'\n本句要点 not just for one election cycle, but in a __(35)__ way：not just one cycle 暗\n含'不止一次性'，与之对照的正是'持续地、长期地'，in a sustained way 是英文里强调长期\n坚持的固定写法。\n【竞争词】K) lavish 义为'奢华的'，与时间维度无关\nin a lavish way 形容方式'铺张奢侈'，与'不止一次选举周期'的对照无任何逻辑联系。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 K) —— lavish 与 way 搭配看似通顺。in a lavish way 字面读起来\n似乎成立，考生不细看上下文易误选；防坑点是利用 not just A, but B 的对照结构——A\n是 one election cycle（短期），B 必为'长期持续'，sustained 才合方向。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·介词 in + 限定词 a + ___"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Lots of research demonstrates that being socially isolated is harmful to the elderly.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】老年人 + 社交隔离有害\n【定位】Abundant research shows the harms of social isolation, considered a serious public health\nproblem in countries that have rapidly ageing populations (though talk of a 'loneliness\nepidemic' may be exaggerated).\n大量研究显示社交隔离的危害，它被视为快速老龄化国家的一项严重公共卫生问题（尽管\n「孤独流行病」的说法可能夸大其词）。\n【改写】Lots of research demonstrates ↔ Abundant research shows\n同义词替换\nbeing socially isolated is harmful ↔ the harms of social isolation\n形容词结构 → 名词化\nthe elderly ↔ rapidly ageing populations\n概括上位换写\n【辨邻】 D D 段虽然也谈隔离的害处，但范围被锁在「极端且非自愿」的案例：被囚禁者、孤儿院\n儿童、单独监禁的犯人，Examples of people kept in captivity, children kept\nisolated in abusive orphanages, and prisoners kept in solitary confinement，并\n不针对「老年人 the elderly」这一人群，故不是 36 题的源头段。\nprolonged solitude can lead to hallucinations(幻觉) and other forms of mental instability", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Being alone enables artists to think and observe, which is a must for their creation.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】艺术家创作所需的思考与观察\n【定位】Solitude allows for the reflection and observation necessary for that creative process.\n独处为这一创作过程所必需的反思与观察提供了空间。\n【改写】think and observe ↔ reflection and observation\n动词 → 名词化\na must for their creation ↔ necessary for that creative process\n口语化与正式表达互换\nBeing alone enables artists ↔ Solitude allows for\n近义词替换 + 主语让位\n【辨邻】 G G 段是 Feist 的研究结论 Feist's research on both artists and scientists shows that\none of the most prominent features of creative folks is their lesser interest in\nsocialising，并未出现「思考、观察、创作过程所需」的机制陈述。\na lack of concern for social norms", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "To one writer, being forced to play with others was a penalty while being kept alone in a room was quite a pleasure.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】作家：被迫和别人玩＝惩罚，独处＝乐事\n【定位】When parents on TV shows punished their kids by ordering them to go to their rooms, I was\nconfused. I loved my room. Being there behind a locked door was a treat. To me a\npunishment was being ordered to play games with my cousin Louis.\n当电视剧里的父母用「回房间去」来惩罚孩子时，我感到困惑。我爱我的房间。锁着门待在\n里面是一种享受。对我来说，惩罚是被要求和表哥 Louis 一起玩游戏。\n【改写】To one writer ↔ author Anneli Rufus\n上位概括\nbeing forced to play with others was a penalty ↔\na punishment was being ordered to play games with my cousin Louis\n近义词替换\nbeing kept alone in a room was quite a pleasure ↔ Being there behind a locked door was a treat\n近义词替换 + 同景概括\n【辨邻】 A A 段的「我」是文章作者本人，I'm sometimes secretly pleased when social plans\nare called off 与题干的「作家受罚说」无关，未提「被迫与人玩＝惩罚」这一独家\n细节。\nI can be a reluctant socialiser", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Recent research shows for the first time that there is a specific link between being creative and being unsociable.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】首次发现：创造力与不爱社交专门挂钩\n【定位】A paper by Bowker and her colleagues was the first to show that a type of social\nwithdrawal could have a positive effect—they found that creativity was linked specifically\nto unsociability.\nBowker 及同事的一篇论文首次表明，一类社交回避行为可以带来正面效应——他们发现，\n创造力专门与「不爱社交」相关联。\n【改写】Recent research shows for the first time ↔\nA paper by Bowker and her colleagues was the first to show\n副词「first」直接保留\na specific link between being creative and being unsociable ↔\ncreativity was linked specifically to unsociability\n副词 specifically 与形容词 specific 互换 + 形名互换\n【辨邻】 G G 段中 Feist's research on both artists and scientists shows 给的是较早的总体结\n论，并无「首次发现」与「专门挂钩」的表述，主语是 Feist 而非 Bowker。\none of the most prominent features of creative folks is their lesser interest in socialising", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "We must be willing to stay alone at least for some time in order to plot our own course.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】为「走自己的路」而愿意独处\n【定位】To truly chart our own path or vision, we have to be willing to isolate ourselves, at least for\nsome period of time.\n要真正规划我们自己的道路或愿景，我们必须愿意把自己与他人隔离开来，至少一段时间。\n【改写】plot our own course ↔ chart our own path or vision\n罕见同源隐喻短语\nWe must be willing to stay alone at least for some time ↔\nwe have to be willing to isolate ourselves, at least for some period of time\n近义短语对换 + 时间表达保留\n【辨邻】 F F 段只是泛论 Solitude is a time for reflection, for thinking about things more\ndeeply，没有「plot our own course / chart our own path」的目的状语，不是 40\n题的源头段。\nthere are benefits in learning to love your solitude", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "According to new research, for people who prefer being alone, solitude can be beneficial to their work lives and emotional health.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】偏好独处者：工作 + 情绪健康双受益\n【定位】For those of us who just prefer plenty of alone time, emerging research suggests some\ngood news: there are upsides to being alone—for both our work lives and our emotional\nwell-being.\n对于我们这些只是更喜欢有大量独处时间的人来说，新近的研究带来一些好消息：独处对我\n们的工作生活和情绪健康都有正面意义。\n【改写】According to new research, for people who prefer being alone ↔\nFor those of us who just prefer plenty of alone time, emerging research suggests\n近义词替换 + 限定词保留\nsolitude can be beneficial to their work lives and emotional health ↔\nthere are upsides to being alone—for both our work lives and our emotional well-being\n形 / 名互换 + 复合名词保留\n【辨邻】 F F 段以散文化笔调说 Solitude is a time for reflection、something to be\ntreasured，但既无 emerging / new research 这一研究信号，也没把「work lives +\nemotional well-being」并列起来。\nthere are benefits in learning to love your solitude", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "It may turn out to be better for one to cultivate a few steady relationships than to busy themselves socialising.", "options": null, "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】少数稳固关系 ＞ 忙于社交\n【定位】As with many things, quality reigns over quantity. Nurturing a few solid relationships without\nfeeling the need to constantly populate your life with endless chatting ultimately may be\nbetter for you.\n如同许多事一样，质量胜过数量。培育少数几段稳固的关系，而不必感到必须用无休止的闲\n聊把生活填满，从长远看也许对你更好。\n【改写】to cultivate a few steady relationships ↔ Nurturing a few solid relationships\n近义动词 + 近义形容词\nthan to busy themselves socialising ↔\nwithout feeling the need to constantly populate your life with endless chatting\n比较结构同义改写\nIt may turn out to be better for one ↔ ultimately may be better for you\n情态词与「最终」副词保留\n【辨邻】 N N 段说 introverts tend to have fewer but stronger friendships—which has been\nlinked to greater happiness，与本题相近但不带「质胜于量 quality reigns over\nquantity」与「不停闲聊」这两个题干精确锚点。\nintroverts tend to have fewer but stronger friendships", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Allowing your mind to wander freely is conducive to sharpening your focus in the long run.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】放任思绪游走 → 长远更专注\n【定位】Giving free rein to a wandering mind not only helps with focus in the long term but\nstrengthens your sense of both yourself and others.\n放任游走的思绪不仅有助于长期保持专注，还会增强你对自我和他人的感知。\n【改写】Allowing your mind to wander freely ↔ Giving free rein to a wandering mind\n近义习语 + 形名互换\nis conducive to sharpening your focus in the long run ↔ helps with focus in the long term\n近义动词 + 时间副词同义\n【辨邻】 K K 段说 the brain's state of active mental rest, which goes hand-in-hand with the\nstillness of being alone，但「mind to wander freely」「focus in the long run」\n这两个题干关键锚点都在 L 段才出现。\nWhen another person is present, your brain can't help but pay some attention", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Research conducted on artists and scientists indicates that creative people are less interested in hanging out with others.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】对艺术家与科学家做的研究\n【定位】In fact, Feist's research on both artists and scientists shows that one of the most prominent\nfeatures of creative folks is their lesser interest in socialising.\n事实上，Feist 对艺术家与科学家的研究都表明，富有创造力的人最显著的特征之一是对社\n交的兴趣较低。\n【改写】Research conducted on artists and scientists ↔ Feist's research on both artists and scientists\n近义动词 + 研究对象逐字复现\ncreative people are less interested in hanging out with others ↔\none of the most prominent features of creative folks is their lesser interest in socialising\n口语 ↔ 正式词替换 + 比较级保留\n【辨邻】 I I 段是 University at Buffalo psychologist Julie Bowker 的研究，谈的是社交回避的\n三种类型，并未明确说研究对象是 artists and scientists。\nthey found that creativity was linked specifically to unsociability", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "According to Feist, you will have difficulty knowing yourself if you do not stay alone occasionally.", "options": null, "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Feist：不独处便难自知\n【定位】It's hard to examine one's own thoughts, feelings and motives, to be self-aware, and fully\nrelaxed unless you have occasional solitude.\n除非你偶尔独处，否则很难审视自己的想法、感受与动机，做到自我觉察并彻底放松。\n【改写】According to Feist ↔ Feist says\n信源逐字一致\nyou will have difficulty knowing yourself ↔\nIt's hard to examine one's own thoughts, feelings and motives, to be self-aware\n概括上位\nif you do not stay alone occasionally ↔ unless you have occasional solitude\n否定句式等价\n【辨邻】 G G 段中 Feist 的研究指向 lesser interest in socialising，并未提到「不独处便难自\n知」这一论断，故不是 45 题的源头段。\nGregory Feist, who focuses on the psychology of creativity\nPARAGRAPH MAP · 段落主旨图\n个段落各讲了什么\n逐段一句主旨 + 该段最具区分度的「信息指纹」，是逐句回扫定位的工作台。\nA 作者自述是一个有些勉强的社交者，有时取消社交计划反而暗自高兴，甚至参加过十\n天静默冥想营，看中的不是冥想而是静默。\n信息指纹 十天静默冥想营\nB 作者引用 Anneli Rufus 的话：童年时被父母罚回房间不觉得是惩罚，反而觉得被要求\n和表哥 Louis 玩游戏才是惩罚。\n信息指纹 Rufus 童年被罚回房的反向感受\nC 大量研究显示社交隔离有害健康，对快速老龄化国家的老年人尤其严重，孤独感对早\n死的风险等同于糖尿病。\n信息指纹 孤独风险等同糖尿病、老龄化国家\nD 极端社交隔离的案例（被囚禁、孤儿院虐待、单独监禁）会导致幻觉与心理失常。\n信息指纹 极端非自愿隔离与幻觉\nE\n前面讲的是极端且非自愿的隔离，对那些仅仅偏好独处时间的人来说，新研究显示独\n处对工作和情绪都有好处。\n信息指纹 偏好独处者的工作与情绪好处\nF\n独处是反思与深思的时间，在速度与竞争至上的世界里独处尤其珍贵，学会爱上独处\n是有益的。\n信息指纹 独处＝深思与珍贵反思时间\nG\n心理学家 Gregory Feist 把创造力定义为有原创性与有用性的活动，他对艺术家与科学\n家的研究发现，创造性强的人最显著的特征之一是对社交的兴趣较弱。\n信息指纹 Feist 对艺术家与科学家研究、社交兴趣较弱\nH 解释创造性人群较少社交的原因：他们花长时间独自打磨技艺，并要通过艺术表达内\n心世界，独处提供了创作所需的反思与观察。\n信息指纹 独处提供创作所需的反思与观察\nI Bowker 把社交回避分为羞怯、回避、不爱社交三类，她的论文首次显示创造力专门与\n「不爱社交」这一类相关，且不爱社交者不会更具攻击性。\n信息指纹 Bowker 首次显示创造力与「不爱社交」专门关联\nJ 独处对领导力也重要：一项 2011 年披萨连锁店研究显示，员工主动型时内向老板效\n益更高，因为内向者不被强势提议威胁、更愿倾听。\n信息指纹 披萨连锁店内向领导研究\nK 古人早已意识到隔离与心智专注的关联，宗教隐士传统视独处为开悟之要；近期研究\n解释了机制——独处带来主动心智休息，他人在场会必然分散注意。\n信息指纹 宗教隐士传统与心智专注\nL 没有干扰时的走神会激活大脑默认模式网络，帮助巩固记忆与理解他人情绪；放任思\n绪游走长远反而有助于专注，也强化对自我与他人的感知。\n信息指纹 默认模式网络与走神长远助专注\nM Susan Cain 主张深度独处对创造性必不可少：今天人们以为创造来自不拘束的社交，\n其实创造需要持续注意与深度专注；要真正走自己的路，必须愿意独处一段时间。\n信息指纹 Susan Cain 与「走自己的路需独处」\nN 有用独处与危险孤立的界线模糊；Feist 警告「从不独处的人才真危险」，缺少独处难\n以审视自我；且内向者朋友少而牢，与更高幸福感相关。\n信息指纹 Feist「从不独处才危险」、内向者朋友少而牢\nO 如同许多事一样，质量胜过数量；维持少数稳固的关系、而不必不停地用闲聊填满生\n活，最终可能对人更好。\n信息指纹 质重于量、少数稳固关系\nP\n若个性偏向不爱社交，不必强求改变，前提是仍有规律社交、是主动选择独处、有几\n个好友、独处对你身心或工作有益。\n信息指纹 不爱社交不必强行改变", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What do we learn about successful changes in organizations?", "options": ["A) They can be immediately implemented with great ease.", "B) They are usually led by organization leaders of vision.", "C) They call for enthusiastic support from the workforce.", "D) They often result from simulation learning experiences."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do we learn about successful changes in organizations? 是对客观信息的直\n接询问，没有 infer/imply 等推断信号，也没有 main idea/title 等主旨信号，属于典型细\n节题。\n【拆句】What do we learn about successful changes in organizations? 是 What 引导的特殊疑\n问句，主干 we learn，about 介词短语限定 learn 的内容，successful changes 是真正的\n题眼，in organizations 是发生场域。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】That is largely because successful change requires more than a vision, it requires a workforce\nthat not only doesn't resist change, but embraces it.\n这在很大程度上是因为成功的变革不只需要愿景，它还需要一支不但不抵触变革、还会拥抱\n变革的员工队伍。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nThey can be immediately implemented with great ease. 称变革「轻易立刻落地」，\n但 Most organizations aspire to deploy significant change programs, only to find\nthem nearly impossible to implement 明说大多数变革极难落地，两者完全相反。\nB) 偷换概念\nThey are usually led by organization leaders of vision. 把成功变革的关键归到「有\n远见的领导」上，但定位句 successful change requires more than a vision 恰恰说仅\n有 vision 不够，关键在 workforce；把强调点偷换了。\nC) ✓ 正确\nit requires a workforce that not only doesn't resist change, but embraces it ↔\nThey call for enthusiastic support from the workforce.\na workforce ↔ the workforce\nThey call for enthusiastic support from the workforce. 的「require + 全员热情支\n持」与定位句 requires a workforce that ... embraces it 精准对位，且对位完整、无外\n加成分。\nD) 强加因果\nThey often result from simulation learning experiences. 说成功变革「源于」模拟\n学习，但全文逻辑是 simulation 服务于 change，并非 change 的来源；定位句也未涉\n及 simulation。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What should learning leaders do to arouse learners' passion for change?", "options": ["A) Allow them to see what could possibly be achieved.", "B) Help them break free from their old paradigms.", "C) Encourage them to rethink their thought models.", "D) Stimulate them to embrace fresh opportunities."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What should learning leaders do to arouse learners' passion for change? 在问主\n体的具体动作，答案对应原文里一句明确的「该做什么」，属细节题。\n【拆句】What should learning leaders do to arouse learners' passion for change? 主干 What\nshould learning leaders do，to arouse learners' passion for change 是目的状语，for\nchange 进一步修饰 passion。结构清晰，无陷阱。\n【定位】To create this kind of widespread passion, learning leaders must expose the workforce to\nwhat could be, which will enable them to rethink their mental models, enable them to break\nfree from their deep-rooted paradigms and embrace the opportunity to learn.\n为了激发这种广泛的热情，learning leaders 必须把员工带去看「事情本可以是什么样」，\n这将使他们重新审视心智模型、挣脱根深蒂固的旧范式，并拥抱学习机会。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nexpose the workforce to what could be ↔\nAllow them to see what could possibly be achieved.\nthe workforce ↔ them\n预判落在主句动作 expose…to what could be 上，A 用 allow them to see what\ncould possibly be achieved 与之一一对应。\nB) 答非所问\nHelp them break free from their old paradigms. 取自定位句后面的 which 从句中的\n并列动作 enable them to break free from their deep-rooted paradigms，那是\nexpose 之后的结果，而非 learning leaders 直接「应做」的动作。\nC) 答非所问\nEncourage them to rethink their thought models. 取自 which 从句中的 rethink\ntheir mental models，依然属于 expose 之后的结果，不是题干所问的直接动作。\nD) 答非所问\nStimulate them to embrace fresh opportunities. 改写自 embrace the opportunity\nto learn，仍是 which 结果从句尾部的并列结果之一，不是题干问的 learning leaders\n的直接动作。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What does the passage say about lectures, training programs and workshops?", "options": ["A) They are generally incapable of changing workers' behaviors on the job.", "B) They are interventions different from simulations in creating stimuli for change.", "C) They aim at transforming the behaviors of the workers in an organization.", "D) They help managers achieve the full effect of employee development efforts."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the passage say about lectures, training programs and workshops? 是\nsay about + 主体的标准细节题问法，无推断信号。\n【拆句】What does the passage say about lectures, training programs and workshops? 主干\nWhat does the passage say，about 引出讨论对象，对象是三项并列名词。无从句、无否\n定，无陷阱。\n【定位】Lectures, training programs and workshops can explain the intellectual elements of\ntransformation, but they are seldom effective at getting to the behavioral aspects that lie at the\nheart of a significant change initiative.\n讲座、培训项目和工作坊能讲清变革的认知层面，但它们在触及行为层面（而行为层面恰是\n重大变革的核心）这件事上几乎无效。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthey are seldom effective at getting to the behavioral aspects ↔\nThey are generally incapable of changing workers' behaviors on the job.\nthe behavioral aspects that lie at the heart of a significant change initiative ↔\nworkers' behaviors on the job\n预判「改不了行为」直接对应 generally incapable of changing workers'\nbehaviors，两端的否定强度通过 seldom↔generally not 桥接成立。\nB) 无中生有\nThey are interventions different from simulations in creating stimuli for change.\n把三者说成「在制造变革刺激上不同于 simulation 的干预」，原文只把它们与\nsimulation 在「能不能触及行为」上作对照，未把它们也定性为 intervention，更未谈\n它们「创造刺激」。\nC) 与原文相反\nThey aim at transforming the behaviors of the workers in an organization. 称它们\n「以改变行为为目标」，与原文「在行为层面 seldom effective」的判定方向相反——\n原文恰是说它们做不到这件事。\nD) 张冠李戴\nThey help managers achieve the full effect of employee development efforts. 让\n三者「帮助管理者获得员工发展投入的全部成效」，但原文 managers rarely see the\nfull effect of their employee development efforts 是说在常规工作里 managers\n几乎看不到全部成效，且这一判断是关于「常规工作条件」整体，不是三者帮助下的\n状况。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What makes simulation one of the most fruitful forms of learning?", "options": ["A) Its capability of saving time by accelerating the immersive learning experience.", "B) Its potential for learners to examine their skills, knowledge and learning process.", "C) Its capability of providing all participants with a practical learning experience.", "D) Its potential for learners to explore, experiment and practice without any risk."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What makes simulation one of the most fruitful forms of learning? 用「What makes\nX Y?」结构直问 simulation 之所以 fruitful 的原因，原文给出明确归因。\n【拆句】What makes simulation one of the most fruitful forms of learning? make 引导\nsb./sth. + 宾补结构，simulation 是宾语，one of the most fruitful forms of learning 是\n宾补；fruitful 与原文 productive 同义。无陷阱。\n【定位】The ability to explore, experiment and repeatedly apply new knowledge in unlimited, risk-\nfree models is what makes simulation one of the most productive forms of learning.\n在数量无限、零风险的模型里探索、实验并反复运用新知的能力，正是让 simulation 成为\n最高效学习方式之一的原因。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nIts capability of saving time by accelerating the immersive learning experience.\n把 accelerates time 拎出来当主因，但原文里它只是 simulation 优点的描述之一，不\n是 is what makes 强调结构所归的原因。\nB) 偷换概念\nIts potential for learners to examine their skills, knowledge and learning process.\n取自 skills, processes and knowledge all can be highlighted，但原文是 processes\n（业务流程/作业过程），选项改成了 learning process（学习过程），主体偷换；且原\n文是 simulation 来 highlight，不是 learners 来 examine。\nC) 以偏概全\nIts capability of providing all participants with a practical learning experience. 用\npractical learning experience 模糊概括 simulation，未含 risk-free、reflect、\nexperiment 等核心元素；原文 is what makes 主语处也并未用 practical 来归因。\nD) ✓ 正确\nThe ability to explore, experiment and repeatedly apply new knowledge ↔\nIts potential for learners to explore, experiment and practice without any risk.\nin unlimited, risk-free models ↔ without any risk\nThe ability ↔ Its potential\nD 保留了原文的三动词并列与 risk-free 条件，是唯一同时满足两项核心的选项。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What should participants do in a simulation to reap the greatest benefits possible?", "options": ["A) Take the experience as a mere game.", "B) Apply promptly what they learn to their jobs.", "C) Develop a deep level of understanding.", "D) Strive to connect closely with their leaders."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What should participants do in a simulation to reap the greatest benefits possible?\n是问明确做法的标准细节题。\n【拆句】What should participants do in a simulation to reap the greatest benefits possible?\n主干 What should participants do，in a simulation 是地点状语，to reap the greatest\nbenefits possible 是目的不定式，possible 后置修饰 benefits。无陷阱。\n【定位】To maximize benefits from simulation, participants should immediately apply the learning\nfrom the experience to forge a smooth link between learning and doing.\n为最大化从模拟中获得的收益，参与者应立刻把从体验中所学应用到工作中，以打通「学」\n与「做」之间的桥梁。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nTake the experience as a mere game. 让参与者「把体验当作单纯的游戏」，但 If\nthey don't, they will view the experience as a game, which can be difficult to\napply on the job 明确把「视为游戏」当作负面后果。\nB) ✓ 正确\nparticipants should immediately apply the learning from the experience ↔\nApply promptly what they learn to their jobs.\nto forge a smooth link between learning and doing ↔ to their jobs\nB 在副词（promptly ↔ immediately）、核心动作（apply what they learn ↔ apply\nthe learning）与落点（to their jobs ↔ between learning and doing）三处与定位句\n完全对位。\nC) 答非所问\nDevelop a deep level of understanding. 取自 enable individuals and groups to\ndevelop a deep level of understanding，那是 simulation 的功效（果），不是参与\n者「应当做什么以最大化收益」（因）。\nD) 无中生有\nStrive to connect closely with their leaders. 让参与者「努力与领导紧密联系」，但全\n文从未要求 participants 去与 leaders 建立紧密联系，原文 forge a link 的对象是\nlearning 与 doing，不是 participants 与 leaders。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the passage say is the more recent thinking of economic growth?", "options": ["A) It should be made sustainable.", "B) It is vital to the future of humanity.", "C) It should be governments' chief concern.", "D) It is an indicator of government performance."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the passage say is the more recent thinking of economic growth\\? 问「文\n章说……是什么」，要的是原文给出的具体表述，不是要考生推断。\n【拆句】主干是 What does the passage say is the more recent thinking of economic\ngrowth\\?，what 是 say 的宾语，is 引导的从句作 say 的真正宾语；不要把 is 误读成主句\n谓语。结构清晰、无陷阱。\n【定位】Unlimited growth is not sustainable, and economic thinking is moving toward the idea that\nwe should aim for sustainability in our economic models.\n无限增长不可持续，经济思潮正转向「我们应当在经济模型中以可持续为目标」这一理念。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nwe should aim for sustainability in our economic models ↔ It should be made sustainable\n预判「应追求可持续」与 It should be made sustainable 字面同向，无需推断，直接\n锁定。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文说「可持续经济」对未来重要 a sustainable economy is vital to our future，主\n语是「sustainable economy」，而非 It（指 economic growth）本身。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文明确 GDP growth … should not be the primary goal of governments，作者主\n张增长不应是政府首要关切，It should be governments' chief concern 正好相反。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文首句即否定 GDP growth is not a good indicator of how well a country is\nperforming，It is an indicator of government performance 正是这个被否定的旧\n观点。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "Why are some people opposed to the idea that governments should focus on happiness?", "options": ["A) Governments cannot impose happiness on citizens.", "B) People's happiness is built upon their own endeavor.", "C) Happiness means different things to different people.", "D) Happiness depends on sustainable economic growth."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】Why are some people opposed to the idea that governments should focus on\nhappiness\\? 问「反对的原因」，原文必有一段集中陈述反对理由。\n【拆句】主干 Why are some people opposed to the idea，后接 that 引导的同位语从句解释\nidea 的内容；opposed to 是固定搭配「反对」，不要看成被动语态。\n【定位】Even on the broadest interpretation of 'happiness' as prosperity or 'life satisfaction', people\nwant different things.\n即使把「幸福」理解到最宽泛的程度——视为「繁荣」或「生活满意度」——人们想要的东\n西也各不相同。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nGovernments cannot impose happiness on citizens 是作者本人的让步\n（governments cannot impose life satisfaction on citizens），而非反对者的反对原\n因，时空错位。\nB) 无中生有\n原文 P2 从未提到「幸福建立在个人努力上」；endeavor 在全文不存在，是凭空添加的\n因果。\nC) ✓ 正确\npeople want different things ↔ Happiness means different things to different people\nDon't different people experience different levels of happiness\\? ↔\nHappiness means different things to different people\n「幸福因人而异」与 Happiness means different things to different people 同义，\n三个反问句的共同指向皆收束于此。\nD) 强加因果\nP1 把 sustainable 与 happiness 视为两条不同线索，原文从未说 Happiness depends\non sustainable economic growth；该选项把两个概念强行嫁接。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What does philosopher Julian Baggini suggest governments do in a recently published article?", "options": ["A) Try to reduce inequality between the rich and the poor.", "B) Provide people with access to resources for a better life.", "C) Change people's behaviors to put social wealth to better use.", "D) Make use of advanced technologies to improve people's lives."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does philosopher Julian Baggini suggest governments do 是经典「某人的具体\n主张」型细节题。\n【拆句】主干 What does philosopher Julian Baggini suggest governments do，suggest 后接\n省略 that 的宾语从句 governments do (what)，what 是 do 的宾语前置——理解为\n「Baggini 建议政府做什么」。\n【定位】Access is key: people do not need to own, but rather access things that enable them to\nlive well.\n「使用权」是关键：人们不必拥有，而是要能用上那些让他们生活得更好的东西。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\ncould help reduce inequality 只是 Baggini 主张的「附带效益」（could help），不\n是其建议政府该做的核心；选项 A 把附带效益当成主张主体。\nB) ✓ 正确\naccess things that enable them to live well ↔\nProvide people with access to resources for a better life\n预判「让公民能用上让生活更好的资源」与 Provide people with access to resources\nfor a better life 字面同向。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文说 changes in social behavior enable us to make more efficient use of the\nassets，是「社会行为已发生的变化」让现有资产用得更高效；选项把它改成「政府去\n改变人们行为以更好利用社会财富」，主动者与目的都被偷换。\nD) 以偏概全\nTechnological advances 在原文同样只是 access 模型成立的「条件」，不是 Baggini\n给政府的建议；选项 D 抓了边缘条件而漏掉主张句。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "Why does the WHO feel the need to appeal to the economic benefits of improving mental health to justify its recommendations?", "options": ["A) Mental health programs cannot be executed without GDP growth.", "B) Psychological interventions are conducive to people's wellbeing.", "C) Poor mental health is detrimental to a nation's economic system.", "D) Governments still take economic development as their priority."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （推断题）\n【判型】Why does the WHO feel the need to appeal to the economic benefits 问「WHO 为何\n觉得有必要诉诸经济利益」，答案不在 WHO 这句的字面，而要从作者紧接的评论中推断政\n府的倾向。\n【拆句】主干 Why does the WHO feel the need to appeal to the economic benefits of\nimproving mental health to justify its recommendations\\?，feel the need to do = 觉\n得有必要做，to justify 是目的状语——「为了让其建议显得合理」。\n【定位】It says a lot, however, that the WHO feels the need to appeal to the economic benefits of\nimproving mental health to persuade governments that the cost of taking proposed measures\nis justified. As long as the economy is their priority, governments need go no further than\nensuring citizens' continued productivity.\n然而，WHO 不得不诉诸「改善心理健康带来的经济收益」来说服政府这一举措的成本合\n理，这一点本身就说明了问题：只要政府仍把经济置于首位，他们就只关心公民能否继续创\n造产值。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文从未说「无 GDP 增长就执行不了心理健康项目」，仅说「政府要看到经济收益才肯\n掏钱」；选项 A 把动机改成了执行前提，凭空捏造。\nB) 答非所问\nPsychological interventions are conducive to people's wellbeing 是事实陈述，原文\nimproving mental health 也支持这一点，但题干问的是「WHO 为何要诉诸经济收\n益」，与「干预有益健康」无关。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文强调「政府以经济为先」是导致 WHO 必须用经济话术的「原因」；选项 C 反过来\n说「心理健康差对经济不利」是 WHO 用经济话术的原因——把工具变成了动机来源。\nD) ✓ 正确\nAs long as the economy is their priority ↔\nGovernments still take economic development as their priority\nIt says a lot, however, that the WHO feels the need to appeal to the economic benefits ↔\nGovernments still take economic development as their priority\n「政府只听经济话术」的根本原因即「政府仍以经济为先」，与 Governments still\ntake economic development as their priority 同义。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-3-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What message does the author try to convey at the end of the passage?", "options": ["A) Governments' goal should be prosperity-driven.", "B) Governments' goal should be people-oriented.", "C) Governments should consider citizens' views in decision-making.", "D) Governments should set sustained productivity as their top priority."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （主旨题）\n【判型】What message does the author try to convey at the end of the passage\\? 问作者结尾\n要传达的核心信息，是局部主旨题。\n【拆句】主干 What message does the author try to convey at the end of the passage\\?，try\nto convey = 试图传达；message 是宾语前置至 what。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n全文反对把繁荣/产值当目标（should not be the primary goal / ensuring citizens'\ncontinued productivity 被作者批评），Governments' goal should be prosperity-\ndriven 正好是作者反对的立场。\nB) ✓ 正确\nview citizens as ends in themselves ↔ Governments' goal should be people-oriented\nset the 'happiness' of citizens as their highest priority ↔\nGovernments' goal should be people-oriented\n末段「把公民视为目的」是全文落点，与 Governments' goal should be people-\noriented 同义。\nC) 无中生有\n原文从未谈及「决策时听取公民意见」（views in decision-making）；这是把「以人\n为本」误解为「问政于民」，添加了原文不存在的程序性内容。\nD) 与原文相反\n作者在 governments need go no further than ensuring citizens' continued\nproductivity 中明显嘲讽了「以产值为先」的政府逻辑；sustained productivity as\ntheir top priority 正是作者批评的对象。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "主旨题"]}], "listeningSharedWith": "2025-06-2"});
